% ============================================================================
% morse-code-origin — the code system Morse code's letter/pattern table is
% built from → who proposed the much-improved code it derives from and when,
% decoded from a span already sitting unused inside the SAME source
% `morse-code.adj` already cites (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `morse-code.adj` (`morse_code(letter,
% pattern)`) and `morse-code-standard.adj` (`morse_code_standard(code_system,
% standard_id)`): a new `morse_code_origin(code_system, originator, year)`
% table names who proposed the improved code the international standard was
% derived from, and when, decoded from a clause already sitting unused
% inside `morse-code.adj`'s own `source` field -- no new WebFetch. That
% table's schema has room for only a letter and its pattern; the
% already-quoted sentence also names the originator and year, which never
% made it into a row:
%
%   international_morse_code → friedrich_gerke, 1848
%     "The Morse code, as specified in the current international standard,
%      International Morse Code Recommendation, ITU-R M.1677-1, was derived
%      from a much-improved proposal by Friedrich Gerke in 1848."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? morse_code_origin(international_morse_code, $Originator, $Year)  % friedrich_gerke, 1848
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a THREE-column table -- the
% quoted sentence names two distinct facts about the origin, originator AND
% year, that belong together as one event rather than two separate sibling
% tables): the single `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `morse_code_origin(code_system, originator, year)` carrying the citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine
% returns the originator and year WITH their source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on any code system that is not the one tabled
% here (it never invents an originator or year). The query also runs
% BACKWARD -- bind an originator and recall which code system they proposed:
%
%     ? morse_code_origin($CodeSystem, friedrich_gerke, $Year)  % international_morse_code, 1848
%
% This is honestly a ONE-ROW table: the already-quoted sentence names
% exactly one code system's origin story. A recall on a different, real
% code system this quote does NOT name -- `american_morse_code`, a
% historically distinct Morse variant used on US telegraph lines before the
% international standard, the same abstention target `morse-code-standard.
% adj` already uses -- correctly abstains rather than inventing an
% originator or year for it.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME span already quoted inside
% `morse-code.adj`'s own `source` field (and already reused, verbatim, by
% the sibling `morse-code-standard.adj`), from the SAME Wikipedia "Morse
% code" article that table already cites -- no new WebFetch, only a
% different schema decoding a different part of that already-verified
% quote. `trust consensus` -- the same tier both siblings already carry (an
% encyclopedia reproducing the ITU chart is a secondary reference, not the
% primary ITU document itself).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table morse_code_origin {
    columns code_system, originator, year

    row (international_morse_code, friedrich_gerke, 1848)

    source "The Morse code, as specified in the current international standard, International Morse Code Recommendation, ITU-R M.1677-1, was derived from a much-improved proposal by Friedrich Gerke in 1848."
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morse_code"
    trust consensus
}
